WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.920 --> 00:00:06.330
We are now ready to classify proteins
into families using Hidden Markov Models.

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:09.130
Let's assume that we are
given the multiple alignment

00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:12.590
of already known members
of the protein family

00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:16.690
and that we have constructed
an HMM based on this family.

00:00:16.690 --> 00:00:19.440
And now, a new protein comes in, and

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:24.230
we want to decide whether it
belongs to the given protein family.

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:28.950
The easiest thing to do is simply apply
the Viterbi algorithm to find an optimal

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:34.600
hidden path and decide
whether this protein belongs

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:37.850
to the family depending on the
probability threshold for this protein.

00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:40.245
So that's what shown on this slide.

00:00:40.245 --> 00:00:45.780
And if the probabilistic threshold

00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:53.490
is satisfied, then we classify the protein
as belonging to this family and

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:59.340
extend the alignment of proteins in
this family as shown in this slide.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:05.420
So, to do this classification, we
need to construct the Viterbi graph for

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:07.710
the given HMM diagram.

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:10.240
Please note that we have not discussed yet

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:14.130
how to construct the Viterbi graph for
an HMM diagram.

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:19.090
For example, how many rows
will this Viterbi graph have?

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:23.760
Well, as before, the number of rows
in the Viterbi graph should be

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:27.710
equal to the number of
states in this HMM diagram.

00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:34.410
So, a large number of rows. And
here, I showed the Viterbi graph, and

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:39.460
I took the liberty to assign
every single column

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:44.040
to every state the HMM is passing through.

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:44.920
Does it make sense?

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:50.060
So, the number of columns in
the Viterbi graph shown here

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:53.150
is equal to the number of visited states.

00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:57.410
There's one problem with
this Viterbi graph:

00:01:57.410 --> 00:02:00.650
We don't know in advance what will be

00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:05.490
the number of visited states when we
look at the newly sequenced protein.

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:09.630
And therefore, it's not even clear
how to construct this graph.

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:12.290
For example, in this case, it
has two deletion states, but

00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:18.350
how do we know this before
we constructed the Viterbi graph?

00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:24.330
And therefore, we should actually
try to think about how to construct

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:30.485
the Viterbi graph with the number
of columns based on the number of

00:02:30.485 --> 00:02:35.175
emitted symbols, something that we know,
rather than the number of visited states,

00:02:35.175 --> 00:02:40.560
something that we don't know in the case
of silent states such as deletion states.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:45.394
And therefore, by definition,
the number of columns in the Viterbi

00:02:45.394 --> 00:02:49.615
graph should be equal to
the number of emitted symbols.

00:02:49.615 --> 00:02:54.357
Which means it should be smaller
than the number of columns

00:02:54.357 --> 00:02:58.432
in this erroneously
constructed Viterbi graph.

00:02:58.432 --> 00:03:02.770
But here is the correctly
constructed Viterbi graph.

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:07.500
Every time we move to the deletion
state and do not emit a symbol,

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:12.740
we actually transform the edge into
a vertical edge within the same column.

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:19.160
In this case,
the number of columns in the Viterbi graph

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:24.460
is equal to the number of emitted symbols,
and the Viterbi then is well-defined.

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:29.440
The only thing we need to
add is the zero column

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:32.855
that contains only silent states for
technical details.

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:39.470
So we just reduced
the problem of alignment

00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:44.670
with a profile HMM to the following
problem: Align a new sequence

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:49.310
to a family of of aligned
sequences using the profile HMM.

00:03:49.310 --> 00:03:55.030
The input is a multiple alignment,
the string text, a threshold "theta"

00:03:55.030 --> 00:04:01.580
describing the maximum fraction of insertions
per column, and a pseudocount "sigma".

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:08.306
The output is an optimal hidden path
emitting text in the profile HMM,

00:04:08.306 --> 00:04:13.130
denoted HMM Alignment, Theta, Sigma.

00:04:13.130 --> 00:04:18.860
Let's take a look at the hidden path
constructed in the HMM diagram,

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:23.510
and of course this hidden path
corresponds to a path in

00:04:23.510 --> 00:04:26.200
the usual alignment graph
that we considered before.

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:33.310
The question then arises: We just described
a rather complex decoding algorithm for

00:04:33.310 --> 00:04:36.640
constructing the hidden path in the HMM.

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:41.080
But, wouldn't be easier to just
construct the same alignment pass

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:43.280
using the traditional alignment technique?

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:46.770
Or, in other words,
have I wasted your time?

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:49.520
Indeed, the recurrence for
the Viterbi algorithm

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:54.390
is very similar to the recurrence for
the traditional sequence alignment.

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:59.072
For the Viterbi Algorithm, the score
node is equal to the maximum of three

00:04:59.072 --> 00:05:01.720
scores of the preceding state.

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:04.890
For the alignment,
again the score of the node

00:05:04.890 --> 00:05:09.030
is equal to the maximum of the three
scores of it's predecessor.

00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:12.920
So it indeed looks like
I've wasted your time.

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:17.130
However, notice that
the choice of alignment paths

00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:21.710
is now based on varying transition and
emission probabilities

00:05:21.710 --> 00:05:27.330
within different columns, and therefore,
this individual scoring parameter

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:32.760
allows us to detect subtle
similarities using HMMs,

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:37.173
similarities that the traditional
sequence alignment often misses.

